d token_labels.\n\nThe goal: project word-level labels onto subword tokens. For each word (group of token indices), broadcast the word's label to each token index. Then token_labels is per token (0/1). kept_indices = [i for i, lbl in enumerate(token_labels) if lbl == 1].\n\nValidation: \n- word_token_indices must have same length as word_labels.\n- Each token index must be in [0, token_count-1].\n- No overlapping: each token index should appear exactly once across all words? Actually \"Reject overlapping, out-of-range, or uncovered token indices.\" Overlapping means same token index appears in multiple word groups. Also uncovered: some token indices not covered by any word? Requirement: \"no_unlabeled_tokens\" – all tokens must be assigned a label. So token indices from 0 to token_count-1 must each appear exactly once across the words. Also order: \"stable_order\" – I assume we just maintain order as given. The word_token_indices should be in order? Probably they are given in order of words; tokens within a word are subword spans that are contiguous? The description says \"broadcast each word label to every token index in that group\". So we just map.\n\nImplement solve(case) that extracts payload fields, validates, computes.\n\nReturn structure: {\"kept_indices\": [...], \"token_labels\": [...]}.\n\nRaise ValueError if invalid.\n\nWe need to output JSON with key \"implementation\" containing the Python source string. Ensure proper escaping.\n\nWrite code. Use standard library.\n\nPseudo:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    # Extract fields\n    wti = case.get(\"word_token_indices\")\n    wl = case.get(\"word_labels\")\n    tc = case.get(\"token_count\")\n    # Validate types and lengths\n    if not (isinstance(wti, list) and isinstance(wl, list) and isinstance(tc, int)):\n        raise ValueError(\"Invalid types\")\n    if len(wti) != len(wl):\n        raise ValueError(\"word_token_indices and word_labels length mismatch\")\n    # Initialize token_labels with None or -1\n    token_labels = [None] * tc\n    # Iterate over words\n    for word_idx, (indices, label) in enumerate(zip(wti, wl)):\n        if not isinstance(indices, list):\n            raise ValueError(\"Invalid token indices list\")\n        for idx in indices:\n            if not isinstance(idx, int) or idx < 0 or idx >= tc:\n                raise ValueError(f\"Invalid token index {idx}\")\n            if token_labels[idx] is not None:\n                raise ValueError(f\"Overlapping token index {idx} at word {word_idx}\")\n            token_labels[idx] = label\n    # Check all tokens covered\n    if any(lbl is None for lbl in token_labels):\n        raise ValueError(\"Uncovered token indices\")\n    # Compute kept_indices\n    kept_indices = [i for i, lbl in enumerate(token_labels) if lbl == 1]\n    # token_labels already is list of ints\n    return {\"kept_indices\": kept_indices, \"token_labels\": token_labels}\n\nThat's it. Ensure function name is solve.\n\nNow produce JSON output.\n\nWe must escape the Python source string.\n\nI'll write the implementation string. Use triple quotes? In JSON string, we need to escape newlines as \\n, backslashes, quotes. Better to write as a single line with \\n. Or use multiline with \\n. I'll format with explicit \\n.\n\nMake sure to include the function definition and proper indentation.\n\nLet me construct the string.\n\nWrite:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    wti = case.get(\"word_token_indices\")\n    wl = case.get(\"word_labels\")\n    tc = case.get(\"token_count\")\n    # validate types\n    if not (isinstance(wti, list) and isinstance(wl, list) and isinstance(tc, int)):\n        raise ValueError(\"Invalid input types\")\n    if len(wti) != len(wl):\n        raise ValueError(\"Mismatched lengths\")\n    token_labels = [None] * tc\n    for word_idx, (indices, label) in enumerate(zip(wti, wl)):\n        if not isinstance(indices, list):\n            raise ValueError(\"Invalid indices list\")\n        for idx in indices:\n            if not 